1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,874
Do you have jobs you keep doing by hand? Running the tests every morning, checking new pull requests, reacting after every deploy?

2
00:00:08,324 --> 00:00:14,642
Routines let Claude Code do them for you, in the cloud, on a schedule, even when your laptop is closed.

3
00:00:15,092 --> 00:00:20,795
In this video, you'll learn what routines are, when to use them, and how to set one up, step by step.

4
00:00:22,345 --> 00:00:23,436
So, what is a routine?

5
00:00:23,886 --> 00:00:32,374
A routine is a saved Claude Code setup: a prompt, one or more GitHub repositories, and the connectors it may use. You package it once.

6
00:00:32,824 --> 00:00:39,715
Then it runs automatically, on Anthropic's cloud, as a full Claude Code session. Your computer doesn't need to be on.

7
00:00:40,165 --> 00:00:53,250
One naming note: routines are now called scheduled tasks. The old name still appears in the web address, and in the slash routines command. It's a research preview, on Pro, Max, Team and Enterprise plans.

8
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:58,633
A routine starts when one of its triggers fires. There are three kinds.

9
00:00:59,083 --> 00:01:04,109
A schedule: hourly, daily, weekly, or just once, at a set time.

10
00:01:04,559 --> 00:01:11,128
An API trigger: your own script, like a deploy pipeline, calls the routine's URL with a secret token.

11
00:01:11,578 --> 00:01:16,007
And a GitHub trigger: a pull request or a release event on your repository.

12
00:01:16,457 --> 00:01:21,427
And you can combine them. The same routine can run every morning, and after every deploy.

13
00:01:22,877 --> 00:01:24,136
When should you use a routine?

14
00:01:24,586 --> 00:01:33,843
For jobs that are unattended, repeatable, and have a clear outcome. They run in the cloud, on a fresh copy of your GitHub repository, at most once an hour.

15
00:01:34,293 --> 00:01:43,124
If the job needs files on your computer, use a Desktop scheduled task. For quick polling while you work, use slash loop. Both need your machine to be on.

16
00:01:44,574 --> 00:01:48,374
Our use case: a test guard, for my small shop's code on GitHub.

17
00:01:48,824 --> 00:01:57,282
Before: every morning, I open the project and run the tests. I notice a failure only when I remember to check, and only when I'm at my desk.

18
00:01:57,732 --> 00:02:06,945
After: a routine runs the tests every weekday morning, and after every deploy. If something fails, it fixes it on a new branch, and reports what it did.

19
00:02:08,395 --> 00:02:13,550
Here's the project: the cart code, its tests, and a short CLAUDE.md with my rules.

20
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,428
Someone merged a change yesterday. I run the tests by hand, and one fails.

21
00:02:18,878 --> 00:02:23,538
I only found it because I happened to check. Let's make Claude check, every day, without me.

22
00:02:24,488 --> 00:02:27,867
Step one: write the prompt. This is the most important part.

23
00:02:28,317 --> 00:02:39,584
The routine runs unattended. Nobody is watching. So the prompt must be self-contained, and say what success looks like. First: run the tests, and if they pass, report the count.

24
00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:47,016
If a test fails: find the cause, don't touch the tests, fix it on a new claude branch, and report the details.

25
00:02:47,466 --> 00:02:57,492
And a rule for the API trigger: text sent with it arrives in a routine fire payload block, labeled as untrusted. My prompt says to use it only as context.

26
00:02:58,342 --> 00:03:03,116
Step two: create the routine, right from Claude Code, with slash schedule.

27
00:03:03,566 --> 00:03:08,948
I describe it in plain English: every weekday at 9:07, run the test guard prompt.

28
00:03:09,398 --> 00:03:16,458
Claude asks a few follow-up questions: the repository, the prompt, the time. Then it saves the routine to my account.

29
00:03:16,908 --> 00:03:21,804
I confirm, and it's saved: weekdays at 9:07, in my own time zone.

30
00:03:23,254 --> 00:03:28,999
Step three: review it on the web, at claude dot A I, slash code, slash routines.

31
00:03:29,449 --> 00:03:35,955
Here's the routine I made in the terminal. The web, the desktop app, and the CLI all share the same list.

32
00:03:36,405 --> 00:03:44,106
On its page: the schedule, the repository it clones on every run, and the environment, which controls network access.

33
00:03:44,556 --> 00:03:50,822
The default environment is trusted: only an allowlist of package registries and common developer sites.

34
00:03:51,272 --> 00:04:02,571
Now, connectors. All your connectors are included by default, and a routine can use every tool in them, even writes, without asking. My test guard doesn't need any, so I remove them.

35
00:04:03,021 --> 00:04:11,286
Remember: every run acts as you. Commits carry your GitHub name, and connector actions use your accounts. So give it only what it needs.

36
00:04:12,136 --> 00:04:17,381
Step four: add more triggers. I open Edit, and in the trigger row, I click plus.

37
00:04:17,831 --> 00:04:27,681
A GitHub event: when a pull request is opened, and it's not a draft. A pull request run starts from that pull request's code, so the guard tests the change before I merge it.

38
00:04:28,131 --> 00:04:34,170
This needs the Claude GitHub App installed on the repository. The web page asks you to install it if it's missing.

39
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:39,184
Then an API trigger. I copy the fire URL, and generate a token.

40
00:04:39,634 --> 00:04:46,762
The token is shown only once. I store it straight away, in my deploy pipeline's secrets. Never in the repository.

41
00:04:48,112 --> 00:04:51,423
Now my deploy script can start the routine. Here's the script.

42
00:04:55,140 --> 00:05:06,878
A POST to the fire URL, with the token as a bearer header, and the routines beta header. Both come from secrets, not the file. The text field adds context, like which version just shipped.

43
00:05:07,328 --> 00:05:12,167
I run it after a deploy. The response is a new session, with a link to watch it live.

44
00:05:13,017 --> 00:05:17,237
Step five: check the runs. Here's what happened at 9:07 this morning.

45
00:05:17,687 --> 00:05:23,839
I ask Claude directly: what did my test guard do this morning? Claude reads the run's log, and explains.

46
00:05:24,289 --> 00:05:32,321
The test guard found the failing test, fixed the cause, ran the tests again, and pushed a claude branch. I just review it, and merge.

47
00:05:32,771 --> 00:05:43,213
One warning: in the runs list, a green status only means the session started and exited. It doesn't mean the work succeeded. Always open the run, and read what Claude did.

48
00:05:44,763 --> 00:05:46,469
Four tips before you automate more.

49
00:05:46,919 --> 00:05:55,494
One: scope what it can reach. Runs have no permission prompts, so choose only the repositories, network access, and connectors the job needs.

50
00:05:55,944 --> 00:06:00,598
Two: schedule a few minutes past the hour, because on-the-hour runs can start late.

51
00:06:01,048 --> 00:06:08,413
Three: treat API text as data. It arrives labeled as untrusted, and your saved prompt decides how to use it.

52
00:06:08,863 --> 00:06:20,724
And four: mind the limits. The minimum interval is one hour, and runs use your subscription, just like normal sessions. Slash schedule also needs a claude dot A I login, not an API key.

53
00:06:22,274 --> 00:06:22,928
Let's recap.

54
00:06:23,378 --> 00:06:30,422
A routine is a saved prompt, repositories, and connectors, that runs in the cloud, even with your laptop closed.

55
00:06:30,872 --> 00:06:36,331
It starts on a schedule, from an API call, or on a GitHub event, or any mix of the three.

56
00:06:36,781 --> 00:06:42,044
Write a prompt that works with nobody watching, give it only what it needs, and always read the runs.

57
00:06:43,494 --> 00:06:49,132
The test guard prompt, the fire script, the demo project, and a cheat sheet are linked in the description.

58
00:06:49,582 --> 00:06:56,413
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

